##DESCRIPTION
##  Find the 3 Lagrange interpolating polynomials and the resulting
## interpolation through 3 points.
##ENDDESCRIPTION

##KEYWORDS('interpolation', 'quadratic')

## DBsubject('Numerical Analysis')
## DBchapter('Interpolation')
## DBsection('Lagrange Interpolation')
## Date('2/3/2018')
## Author('Peter Staab')
## Institution('Fitchburg State University')

DOCUMENT();

loadMacros(
    'PGstandard.pl',     'PGML.pl',
    'parserFunction.pl', 'scaffold.pl',
    'PGcourse.pl'
);

# We use this to ensure that these are needed for the input. The actual
# functions that L0, L1, L2 map to are not important except that they are independent.
parserFunction("L0(x)" => "sqrt(x)+atan(x)");
parserFunction("L1(x)" => "x^2+exp(x)");
parserFunction("L2(x)" => "sin(x)+2sqrt(x)");

# Pick three unique points.
do {
    $x0 = random(-5, 5);
    $x1 = random(-5, 5);
    $x2 = random(-5, 5);
} while (($x1 == $x0) || ($x2 == $x1) || ($x2 == $x0));

$L0 = Formula("(x-$x1)*(x-$x2)/(($x0-$x1)*($x0-$x2))");
$L1 = Formula("(x-$x0)*(x-$x2)/(($x1-$x0)*($x1-$x2))");
$L2 = Formula("(x-$x0)*(x-$x1)/(($x2-$x0)*($x2-$x1))");

$y0 = random(-6, 6);
$y1 = random(-6, 6);
$y2 = random(-6, 6);

$poly = Compute("$y0*L0(x)+$y1*L1(x)+$y2*L2(x)");

Scaffold::Begin();
Section::Begin("Part A: Find the Lagrange Interpolants");

BEGIN_PGML
Let

[`` x_0 = [$x0], \quad x_1 = [$x1], \quad x_2 = [$x2] ``]

Write the 3 Lagrange Polynomials through these points

[`L_{2,0}(x) = `] [____________________________________]{$L0}

[`L_{2,1}(x) = `] [____________________________________]{$L1}

[`L_{2,2}(x) = `] [____________________________________]{$L2}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

The general form of a Lagrange polynomial that is 1 at [`x_j`] and 0
through all [`x_i`] for [`i=1\ldots n, i \neq j`] is

[`` L_{n,j} = \frac{\prod_{i=1,i \neq j}^n (x-x_i)}
{\prod_{i=1,i \neq j}^n (x_j-x_i)} ``]

For [` x_0 = [$x0], \quad x_1 = [$x1], \quad x_2 = [$x2] `],

[`` L_{2,0} = \frac{(x-[$x1])(x-[$x2])}{([$x0]-[$x1])([$x0]-[$x2])} ``]

[`` L_{2,1} = \frac{(x-[$x0])(x-[$x2])}{([$x1]-[$x0])([$x1]-[$x2])} ``]

[`` L_{2,2} = \frac{(x-[$x0])(x-[$x1])}{([$x2]-[$x0])([$x2]-[$x1])} ``]

END_PGML_SOLUTION

Section::End();

Section::Begin('Part B: Find the interpolating polynomial');

BEGIN_PGML

Find the quadratic that passes through the points [`([$x0],[$y0])`],
[`([$x1],[$y1])`], [`([$x2],[$y2])`].  Use a linear combination of L0(x),
L1(x), L2(x) instead of the actual functions above:

[_]{$poly}{25}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION

And using Lagrange polynomials, the interpolating polynomials is

[`` P(x) = \sum_{j=0}^n y_j L_{n,j} ``]

so for this problem,

[`` P(x) = [$y0] L_{2,0}(x) + [$y1] L_{2,1}(x) + [$y2] L_{2,2}(x) ``]


END_PGML_SOLUTION

Section::End();
Scaffold::End();
ENDDOCUMENT();
